就像，作为一个演员，你知道如果他们一直让你回来，这就说明你做对了某些事。

00:08:19.610 --> 00:08:28.889
And I think, not that she didn't have a lot of confidence, but her, your confidence grows, because you're like, oh, they want me to be part of the constellation of characters in this thing.
而且我认为，倒不是说她本来就缺乏自信，但你的自信心会增强，因为你会想，“哦，他们想让我成为这里面众多角色中的一员。”

00:08:28.889 --> 00:08:36.210
And I remember the feeling too, at this point in the season, right in the middle of the season, we knew we couldn't do every single week a mislead of like, is this the mother?
我也记得那种感觉，在这一季的这个时间点，就在季中，我们知道我们不能每星期都搞一次误导，说什么“这是老妈吗？”

00:08:36.210 --> 00:08:37.049
No, it's not, gotcha.
“不，不是，抓到你了吧。”

00:08:37.049 --> 00:08:37.889
Is this the mother?
“这是老妈吗？”

00:08:37.889 --> 00:08:38.730
Is this the mother?
“这是老妈吗？”

00:08:38.730 --> 00:08:39.570
We knew we had to make it count.
我们知道我们得让每次出场都有意义。

00:08:39.570 --> 00:08:44.690
So I remember like the pressure on this, on casting this, because we're like, this is gonna be a real mislead.
所以我记得当时在选角上承受的压力，因为我们在想，这会是一个真正的误导。

00:08:44.690 --> 00:08:46.769
This is really going to be, this could be the mother.
这真的会是——这完全可能是老妈。

00:08:46.769 --> 00:08:52.970
This is someone that has the gravitas and the charm and the wit and the sparkle to be, to potentially be the mother.
这是一个拥有威严、魅力、机智和光芒，可以——潜在成为老妈的人。

00:08:52.970 --> 00:08:55.850
We really wanted people to believe that in the moment.
我们真的很想让观众在那一刻相信这一点。

00:08:56.850 --> 00:08:59.490
And that's a tough, that's a really tough person to find.
这是一个高难度的人物，真的非常难找。

00:08:59.490 --> 00:09:01.129
And you, with Ashley, you just knew it.
而对于阿什莉，你一眼就能确定。

00:09:01.129 --> 00:09:02.090
You knew it right away.
你立刻就能看出来。

00:09:02.090 --> 00:09:04.289
We saw a lot of people and she was just right.
我们见了很多演员，而她就是对的那个人。

00:09:04.289 --> 00:09:06.850
She was just fit in with everybody immediately.
她立刻就和所有人都契合了。

00:09:06.850 --> 00:09:09.570
You could picture her being part of the series, right?
你能想象她成为剧集的一部分，对吧？

00:09:09.570 --> 00:09:15.149
It felt like she could become a series regular, basically, by the end of a couple episodes.
感觉拍完几集之后，她基本上就可以成为剧组的常驻演员了。

00:09:15.149 --> 00:09:29.190
And I wrote you this little day to sing to you in New York City.
我为你写了这首小曲，要在纽约市唱给你听。

00:09:29.190 --> 00:09:32.990
Hi, I'm Alex Goldman, host of the Hyper-Fixed Podcast.
嗨，我是亚历克斯·戈德曼，《Hyper-Fixed》播客的主持人。

00:09:32.990 --> 00:09:36.470
Each week, we take listeners' problems and try to solve them for them.
每周，我们都会收集听众的问题并试着为他们解决。

00:09:36.470 --> 00:09:39.629
Problems like, I'm 30 and I'm scared to drive in New York.
比如这样的问题：我30岁了，但很害怕在纽约开车。

00:09:39.629 --> 00:09:43.429
Or why can't I adjust the volume of my car stereo when I'm in reverse?
或者为什么我在倒车时不能调节汽车音响的音量？

00:09:43.429 --> 00:09:45.950
We also solve non-car related problems.
我们也解决与汽车无关的问题。

00:09:45.950 --> 00:09:52.139
If you have a problem, not only will we fix it, will expose the hidden systems that caused that problem in the first place.
如果你有一个问题，我们不仅会帮你解决，还会揭示导致该问题的背后隐藏系统。

00:09:52.139 --> 00:09:54.659
That's the hyper-fixed podcast from Radiotopia.
这就是来自Radiotopia的《Hyper-Fixed》播客。

00:09:54.659 --> 00:10:12.740
Find it wherever you find podcasts or at hyperfixedpod.com.
你可以在任何收听播客的地方找到它，或者访问 hyperfixedpod.com。

00:10:12.740 --> 00:10:15.220
I'm going to sort of gather up a lot of people's questions.
我打算把大家的很多问题汇总一下。

00:10:15.220 --> 00:10:16.460
I'll shout out some names here.
我在这里念几个名字。

00:10:16.460 --> 00:10:25.259
Ryan Schwartz TV, Jamie Redfern, Terry O'D02.
Ryan Schwartz TV、Jamie Redfern、Terry O'D02。

00:10:25.259 --> 00:10:28.659
A lot of questions right off of what you two are asking.
有很多问题正好切合你们俩刚才探讨的内容。

00:10:28.659 --> 00:10:45.500
Many people have a theory that since this was heading towards the 13th episode, which was the first amount of episodes that were bought right, that a lot of people are asking, were you ready for her to be the mother if you were canceled halfway through?
很多人有一种理论，因为当时正走向第13集，这也是最初购买的集数，对吧，所以很多人都在问，如果你们在半路被砍了，你们准备好让她成为母亲了吗？

00:10:45.500 --> 00:10:46.860
Many people ask this question.
很多人问这个问题。

00:10:46.860 --> 00:10:48.139
I'll just ask that one.
我刚好来问这一个。

00:10:48.139 --> 00:10:49.139
Yes, no.
对，不是。

00:10:49.139 --> 00:10:50.460
With that ever in your thoughts.
你们脑子里有过这种想法吗。

00:10:50.460 --> 00:10:53.379
We had already gotten picked up for the back nine because the show had done well enough.
我们当时已经续订了后九集，因为这部剧的表现足够好。

00:10:53.379 --> 00:10:58.539
But you know, in the back of our heads, we probably did put something big there.
但你知道，在我们脑海深处，我们可能确实在那里安排了一些重磅内容。

00:10:58.539 --> 00:11:05.100
We were thinking if it had to wrap up speedily, we could somehow achieve that if we had to scramble that.
我们在想，如果必须迅速收尾，如果必须临时拼凑，我们也能勉强做到。

00:11:05.100 --> 00:11:08.629
But also it was like, let's put something big right there.
但同时这也像是，我们在那儿搞个大招吧。

00:11:08.629 --> 00:11:09.470
Right?
对吧？

00:11:09.470 --> 00:11:13.909
At the end of these couple of episodes, we had to leave them wanting more.
在这几集的末尾，我们得让观众意犹未尽。

00:11:13.909 --> 00:11:16.309
We had to make the network be like, we made the right decision.
我们得让电视台觉得，他们的决定没有错。

00:11:16.309 --> 00:11:18.070
We were not going to cancel this show.
我们绝不会被砍剧。

00:11:18.070 --> 00:11:19.350
So yeah, that was in our heads.
所以，是的，我们当时就是这么想的。

00:11:19.350 --> 00:11:21.029
It's important to remember back to that, right?
回想过去很重要，对吧？

00:11:21.029 --> 00:11:22.870
We were just a season one show.
我们当时只是一部第一季的剧。

00:11:22.870 --> 00:11:23.870
We didn't know anything.
我们什么都不知道。

00:11:23.870 --> 00:11:26.429
We were not a hit or, you know, we were building.
我们还算不上大热剧，或者说，我们在积累。

00:11:26.429 --> 00:11:27.350
We were finding an audience.
我们在寻找观众。

00:11:27.350 --> 00:11:31.750
And it's a really good question to remember back to those, those were the stakes.
回想过去，这是一个很好的问题，当时的赌注就是这样。

00:11:31.750 --> 00:11:36.629
It's kind of funny also that, that how I met your mother fans are, they're not just lovers of the show.
挺搞笑的一点是，《老爸老妈浪漫史》的粉丝们，他们不仅仅是这部剧的爱好者。

00:11:36.629 --> 00:11:39.750
They, they at this point understand like, oh yeah, they got the initial 13 order.
他们在这个时候已经明白了，哦对，他们最初只订购了13集。

00:11:39.750 --> 00:11:56.830
We don't know if they got the back nine yet, like they're, they're very well, they're well versed in a TV lingo and they understand that like you're structuring, you know, at this point, you really only had one season of television to deal with, you know, you, you might not have gotten more than 22 episodes.
我们不知道他们是否已经拿到了后九集的续订，他们非常懂行，精通电视行业的行话，他们明白当时的剧本结构，你知道的，当时你其实只有一季的剧集可以处理，你可能拿不到超过22集的续订。

00:11:56.830 --> 00:11:59.789
in some ways you have to have the rip cord ready to go.
在某种程度上，你必须随时准备好紧急降落。

00:11:59.789 --> 00:12:04.990
And another thing about that, remembering back to that time is, you had to wait a week to see what the hell was gonna happen.
关于那段时期，还有一点要回忆的是，你得等上一周才能知道到底会发生什么。

00:12:04.990 --> 00:12:12.509
Ted sees Victoria across the crowded room, this amazing shot, this amazing song, boom, you have to wait till the week from then.
泰德在拥挤的房间里看到了维多利亚，这个绝妙的镜头，这首绝妙的歌，轰的一声，你得等上整整一周。

00:12:12.509 --> 00:12:15.070
You have to wait till the next Monday to see the next part of that.
你得等到下周一才能看到接下来的剧情。

00:12:15.070 --> 00:12:18.070
And I can't emphasize enough how we were writing it like that, right?
我怎么强调都不为过，我们当时就是这样写剧本的，对吧？

00:12:18.070 --> 00:12:19.110
This wasn't not a binge show.
这不是一部适合一口气刷完的剧。

00:12:19.110 --> 00:12:20.269
Binging wasn't a thing.
那时候可没有一口气看完全集这种事。

00:12:20.269 --> 00:12:22.070
There wasn't streaming services.
也没有流媒体服务。

00:12:22.070 --> 00:12:25.470
You didn't have every show, every episode you wanted to see, right at your fingertips all the time.
你不会随时随地都能触手可及地看到你想看的每一部剧、每一集。

00:12:25.470 --> 00:12:29.149
And so it was a product of its time and I think in a good way.
所以它是那个时代的产物，而且我认为是以一种很好的方式。

00:12:29.149 --> 00:12:33.110
And I like it when streamers even now make you wait a week between shows.
即使在现在，我也喜欢流媒体平台让观众在剧集之间等待一周。

00:12:33.110 --> 00:12:35.629
I still think that is a good way to do TV.
我依然认为那是制作电视节目的好方法。

00:12:35.629 --> 00:12:41.230
Also, your favorite commercial break bit is Marshall makes popcorn three minutes.
还有，你最喜欢的商业广告时段的梗是马歇尔做爆米花三分钟。

00:12:41.230 --> 00:12:42.230
Yeah.
对。

00:12:42.230 --> 00:12:43.230
With the popcorn.
做爆米花。

00:12:43.230 --> 00:12:44.230
Yeah.
对。

00:12:44.230 --> 00:12:46.909
That moment in China posted and really owned the commercial break.
中国播出的那一刻，真正契合了广告时段。

00:12:46.909 --> 00:12:50.149
That was the landscape of how the show was communicated at that time.
那是当时这部剧传播时的整体环境。

00:12:50.149 --> 00:12:52.029
There was no other way to watch it.
没有别的观看方式。

00:12:52.029 --> 00:12:55.389
It's like people wanted to meet the mother, but it would be pretty anticlimactic.
这就好比人们想要见到老妈，但如果只是那样，就会非常虎头蛇尾。

00:12:55.389 --> 00:12:57.070
If it was just, how do we meet her?
如果只是，我们是怎么认识她的？

00:12:57.070 --> 00:12:58.110
I just met her.
我刚刚才认识她。

00:12:58.110 --> 00:12:59.110
I was like, yeah, yeah, yeah.
我会觉得，啊，好吧，好吧。

00:12:59.110 --> 00:13:00.429
I was like, I'm sorry, I spent her on the wedding.
我会觉得，对不起，我把她花在婚礼上了。

00:13:00.429 --> 00:13:01.269
You know what?
你知道吗？

00:13:01.269 --> 00:13:02.110
I canceled.
我被砍了。

00:13:02.110 --> 00:13:15.139
I do think it's funny there were, at least when the show was on the air, I noticed a lot more people, you know, even coming up to me or journalists asking about it, there was a obsession with like, who's the mother?
我确实觉得很有意思，至少在剧集热播期间，我注意到更多的人，甚至包括来找我的人或记者都在问这个问题，大家有一种执念，那就是：老妈到底是谁？

00:13:15.139 --> 00:13:15.980
Who is it?
是谁？

00:13:15.980 --> 00:13:24.659
Like we, I mean, we've talked about like, this is really a show about a chosen family in New York City going through their 20s and 30s together.
就像我们，我的意思是，我们聊过，这其实是一部关于纽约市的一群挚友共同经历20多岁和30多岁生活的剧集。

00:13:24.659 --> 00:13:26.580
Like that's what the show is broadly.
大致上这就是这部剧的内容。

00:13:26.580 --> 00:13:33.899
But there was this central theme or mystery that you guys were stringing the thing through, the through line of it.
但你们在其中串联起了一个核心主题或悬念，也就是贯穿始终的线索。

00:13:33.899 --> 00:13:44.610
And there are certain people that were so obsessed with that mystery that I don't remember, I don't know, I used to tell people like, well, what if it is like then it's over?
有些人对这个悬念着迷到一定程度，我不记得了，我不知道，我以前常对人说，好吧，如果真的是那样，那剧不就结束了吗？

00:13:44.610 --> 00:13:47.529
Like don't you wanna watch more of this show that you're enjoying?
难道你们不想看更多你们正在享受的剧集吗？

00:13:47.529 --> 00:13:50.090
And if we tell you who the mother is, the show's over.
如果我们告诉你们谁是老妈，剧就结束了。

00:13:50.090 --> 00:13:51.769
So we're not gonna do that right now.
所以我们现在不会这么做。

00:13:51.769 --> 00:13:55.169
Like we're gonna, it's almost like, this is Tantra.
我们要，这简直就像，这就是密宗。

00:13:55.169 --> 00:13:56.809
This isn't like Wambam, thank you.
这可不是那种“完事走人，谢谢”的快餐。

00:13:56.809 --> 00:14:01.409
This is like, we're gonna play with the energy.
这就好比，我们要调动能量。

00:14:01.409 --> 00:14:02.370
We're gonna bring the energy up.
我们要把能量推向高潮。

00:14:02.370 --> 00:14:05.409
We're gonna bring it down, you know, like this is- Alec, this is your clip.
我们要把它降下来，你知道，这就——亚历克，这是你的原话。

00:14:05.409 --> 00:14:06.490
This is your clip.
这是你的原话。

00:14:07.490 --> 00:14:09.129
I'm a true mother.
我是个真正的老妈。

00:14:09.129 --> 00:14:12.250
This is the TV show version of Tantric Sex, yeah.
这就是电视剧版的密宗性爱，对。

00:14:12.250 --> 00:14:13.809
So that's what it is.
所以就是这样。

00:14:14.809 --> 00:14:19.169
That's, look, I mean, you don't say the butler did it in act one of the mystery, right?
就是，你看，你总不能在悬疑剧的第一幕就说管家是凶手吧？

00:14:19.169 --> 00:14:21.730
You can't like, Agatha Christie, like you gotta wait for it.
你不能像阿加莎·克里斯蒂那样，你得耐心等待。

00:14:21.769 --> 00:14:27.929
So, yeah, wait for it is actually the theme of one of the big themes of the show.
所以，是的，“耐性等待”其实是这部剧的一个重大主题。

00:14:27.929 --> 00:14:28.929
Wait for it.
耐心等待。

00:14:28.929 --> 00:14:29.929
Wait for it.
耐心等待。

00:14:29.929 --> 00:14:30.929
Yeah.
对。

00:14:30.929 --> 00:14:31.929
And what's taken so long?
为什么花了这么长时间？

00:14:31.929 --> 00:14:32.929
Hey, let's take it so long.
嘿，为什么花了这么长时间？

00:14:32.929 --> 00:14:37.649
Josh, it's mixed up in all the questions that all those folks asked.
乔希，这夹杂在所有那些人提出的所有问题里。

00:14:37.649 --> 00:14:40.450
Did you do a chemistry read with her?
你和她进行过化学反应试镜吗？

00:14:40.450 --> 00:14:44.009
Does that happen in the midst of a great question, Josh?
乔希，这是在那些精彩问题中发生的吗？

00:14:44.009 --> 00:14:45.009
Do you think we did that?
你觉得我们做过那种事吗？

00:14:45.009 --> 00:14:46.009
Do we do?
我们做过吗？

00:14:46.009 --> 00:14:48.809
Because it was so important that you two be good together.
因为你们俩配合得好实在是太重要了。

00:14:48.809 --> 00:14:51.090
Do you think we brought you into that audition process at all?
你觉得我们有把你拉进那个试镜流程里吗？

00:14:51.090 --> 00:14:53.450
I can't, God, this is old guy theater here.
我记不清了，天哪，这简直是老头子在忆当年了。

00:14:53.450 --> 00:14:54.289
You know what?
你知道吗？

00:14:54.289 --> 00:14:56.250
I can't remember, the year was off five.
我记不清了，那大概是零五年吧。

00:14:56.250 --> 00:14:59.889
Alec, we should ask Ashley that when she comes on the show.
亚历克，等阿什利上节目的时候我们应该问问她。

00:14:59.889 --> 00:15:00.730
Yeah.
对。

00:15:00.730 --> 00:15:01.730
Because I can't remember.
因为我记不得了。

00:15:01.730 --> 00:15:04.409
My feeling is we did not.
我的感觉是我们没有。

00:15:04.409 --> 00:15:16.970
I remember being told that she was going to play the part and she had done a series with a good friend of mine, Mark Foryristine, and I had met her.
我记得当时有人告诉我她要来演这个角色，她之前和我的一位好朋友马克·福里里斯廷合作过一部剧集，我见过她。

00:15:16.970 --> 00:15:20.529
I had seen, I'd been to a taping of this show and I had met her.
我见过，我去看过那部剧的录制，我见过她。

00:15:20.529 --> 00:15:22.889
So I knew who she was, and I knew she was great.
所以我知道她是谁，我也知道她很棒。

00:15:22.889 --> 00:15:31.690
So I was delighted when I heard she was gonna do it, but I don't really remember reading with people, but Ashley might have a different memory, so let's doggear that to ask her.
所以我听说她要出演时非常高兴，但我真的不记得和大家一起试读过剧本，不过阿什利的记忆可能和我的不同，所以我们把这页折起来到时候问她吧。

00:15:31.690 --> 00:15:36.210
So one or two more here, I have them under the title of big questions.
这里还有一两个问题，我把它们归在“重大问题”这个标题下。

00:15:36.210 --> 00:15:41.129
Watching this episode again, this is from Yas Menes Ekri.
再次观看这一集，这是来自亚斯·梅内斯·埃克里的问题。

00:15:41.129 --> 00:15:53.490
Watching this episode again got me thinking, what if the real love story isn't rushing to find someone for the big moments, but trusting that the right person will find you when you're ready, even if that means standing alone at someone else's wedding.
再次观看这一集让我想知道，真正的爱情故事或许不是急于在那些重大时刻找到某人，而是相信只要你准备好了，对的人就会找到你，哪怕这意味着独自站在别人的婚礼上。

00:15:53.490 --> 00:15:54.490
That's a nice thought.
这真是个美好的想法。

00:15:54.490 --> 00:16:01.690
You know, that really, that reminds me of the kind of classic Stella thing of like she's getting here as fast as she can.
你知道，这真的，这让我想起史黛拉那种经典的说法，就像她正以最快的速度赶来。

00:16:01.690 --> 00:16:02.529
Yeah.
对。

00:16:02.529 --> 00:16:03.370
You know?
你知道吗？

00:16:03.370 --> 00:16:04.210
Yeah.
是的。

00:16:04.210 --> 00:16:12.289
To remember that like, the person that you're gonna be with is going on their own kind of crazy, twisty journey.
要记住，就像你将要共度一生的人，她也在经历着属于她自己的、疯狂而又曲折的旅程。

00:16:12.289 --> 00:16:14.690
And it's gonna lead to you.
而这段旅程最终会通向你。

00:16:14.690 --> 00:16:24.809
Both roads are gonna lead to the same destination, So that is an active faith to trust that a person is going through their own ups and downs.
两条道路最终会通往同一个目的地，所以这需要一种积极的信念去相信，一个人正在经历她自己的起起落落。

00:16:24.809 --> 00:16:28.809
They're learning the right lessons they need to learn, but for the time they meet you.
他们在学习需要学习的正确课程，直到与你相遇的那一刻。

00:16:28.809 --> 00:16:29.809
Yeah.
是的。

00:16:29.809 --> 00:16:35.370
It feels like this episode, the star of the end of this episode is kind of the universe in a way.
感觉这一集，这一集结尾的明星某种程度上可以说是宇宙本身。

00:16:35.370 --> 00:16:48.619
They've watched an entire story, the story had a beginning, middle, and end, and then there's this little magic moment at the end that she happens to be there and that sends us into the second half of what feels like a two-parter, but really it's a complete restart right?
他们看完整部故事，故事有头有尾，然后结尾有个神奇的小插曲，她刚好出现在那里，这就把我们带入了感觉像是上下两集故事的后半部分，但实际上这是一个完全的重新开始，对吧？

00:16:48.619 --> 00:16:51.249
It's like we're not going to keep talking about Robin.
就好像我们不会再继续谈论罗宾了。

00:16:51.249 --> 00:16:52.418
We are switching it out.
我们要换个话题了。

00:16:52.418 --> 00:16:54.659
We are now going to be talking about Victoria.
我们现在要开始谈论维多利亚了。

00:16:54.659 --> 00:16:57.289
There's a magic to that moment and it is the universe.
那一刻有一种魔力，这就是宇宙。

00:16:59.129 --> 00:17:14.657
We might want to do a whole episode on this and I don't want to get too deep in the weeds on this, but I think that one of the reasons that how I met your mother resonates so deeply at such a core almost mythic level with people.
我们可能想就此做一整集节目，我不想在这个问题上深究，但我认为《老爸老妈浪漫史》之所以能如此深远地、几乎以一种神话般的内核与人们产生共鸣的原因之一。

00:17:14.657 --> 00:17:34.592
You know, there's a lot of, you know, this is, but like, the history of science and theology, like when the scientific revolution happened and the enlightenment happened and God was kind of plucked out of the center of the universe and it became all about verifiable, provable material reality.
你知道，这里有很多……你知道，这就是，但是，科学和神学的历史，就像科学革命发生和启蒙运动发生时，上帝某种程度上被从宇宙中心驱逐出去，一切都变成了关于可验证、可证明的物质现实。

00:17:34.592 --> 00:17:48.356
And there's this ache at the heart of the modern life, which is like, we long for transcendence, we long for, we're still wired to believe in something beyond that, which we can verify or measure, right?
现代生活的心灵深处有一种痛苦，那就是，我们渴望超越，我们渴望，我们的基因依然让我们相信有什么东西超越了我们可以验证或衡量的东西，对吧？

00:17:48.356 --> 00:17:57.442
And I think that there's something about how I met your mother, someone could write a really good paper on this, and maybe I'll do it.
我认为关于《老爸老妈浪漫史》有一些东西，某人可以为此写一篇非常好的论文，也许我会写。

00:17:57.442 --> 00:18:02.867
But there's something about it puts mystery back at the center of things, right?
但它有一种魔力，把神秘感重新放回了事物的中心，对吧？

00:18:02.867 --> 00:18:08.156
It's not about, it's a lot about what you cannot control, what you cannot see.
这不是关于……它很大程度上关乎你无法控制、无法看到的事情。

00:18:08.156 --> 00:18:13.377
There is some sort of guiding hand that is at play and how I met your mother.
在《老爸老妈浪漫史》中，某种指引之手正在发挥作用。

00:18:13.377 --> 00:18:19.615
And I think it's very satisfying on a soul level to people because we don't want to believe.
我认为这对人们的心灵来说是非常满足的，因为我们不想相信。

00:18:19.615 --> 00:18:24.904
I find a universe that is just material and when we die, it's just lights out.
我发现一个只有物质的宇宙，当我们死后，一切就都熄灭了。

00:18:24.904 --> 00:18:27.887
I find that to be brutalizing to the psyche.
我发现这对心理是一种摧残。

00:18:27.887 --> 00:18:32.227
I find it to be very difficult to exist in a universe like that.
我发现在这样一个宇宙中生存非常困难。

00:18:32.227 --> 00:18:35.685
And I don't actually exist in a universe like that.
而我实际上并不存在于这样一个宇宙中。

00:18:35.685 --> 00:18:37.719
I believe in something bigger.
我信仰某种更伟大的东西。

00:18:37.719 --> 00:18:40.567
I don't want to unpack all that right now.
我不想现在就把所有这些都剖析清楚。

00:18:40.567 --> 00:18:46.398
But I think that part of the magic of how I make a mother is that there's magic in it.
但我认为《老爸老妈浪漫史》的魔力部分在于其中蕴含的魔力。

00:18:46.398 --> 00:18:49.450
It allows the universe to be enchanted again.
它让宇宙再次充满魔法。

00:18:49.450 --> 00:18:52.490
And I think that's very satisfying and relieving to people.
我认为这让人感到非常满足和宽慰。

00:18:52.490 --> 00:18:54.730
Yeah, and I like that a lot.
是的，我也很喜欢这样。

00:18:54.730 --> 00:19:01.650
And at the end of this episode, where Ted is so frenzied and trying to force something into being, something bigger is happening.
在这个节目的结尾，当泰德如此狂热并试图强行促成某事时，更伟大的事情正在发生。

00:19:01.650 --> 00:19:07.210
At the end of this episode that's going to change his life, Future Ted says exactly that at the end, right?
在这集的结尾，这将改变他的人生，年长泰德在结尾时正是这么说的，对吧？

00:19:07.210 --> 00:19:13.609
I just turned out, this sent me in a whole other magical direction I couldn't have known about basically as the ending of that episode.
结果证明，这把我带向了另一个我无法预知的神奇方向，基本上就是那一集的结尾。

00:19:13.609 --> 00:19:20.457
And it's kind of the humbling realization that there are bigger forces that work in our lives and Ted is constantly right there.
这是一种谦卑的觉悟，即在我们的生活中存在着更强大的力量在起作用，而泰德就在那里。

00:19:20.457 --> 00:19:22.971
That's the philosophical question of Ted right?
这就是泰德的哲学问题，对吧？

00:19:22.971 --> 00:19:29.764
Can you force into being and you plan into being versus how much do you have to sort of surrender yourself to something larger?
你能靠强求和计划来达成目标，还是必须在多大程度上将自己臣服于某种更伟大的事物？

00:19:29.764 --> 00:19:42.281
There's some larger story happening and there's a there's almost something comforting in surrendering to that right because Ted you're so good in these episodes Josh at being the the motor you play the kind of the frenzy of Ted right?
某种更宏大的故事正在发生，而臣服于这种故事本身就有一种安慰，对吧？因为乔什，你在这些剧集里真的演得太棒了，你扮演了发动机的角色，你演出了泰德的那种狂热，对吧？

00:19:42.281 --> 00:19:46.989
There's like so there's a bunch of episodes where you are the motor of this show, right?
有很多集里你都是这个节目的发动机，对吧？

00:19:46.989 --> 00:19:49.449
You're making, you're forcing this into being.
你在创造，你在强行促成这一切。

00:19:49.450 --> 00:19:53.906
There's so many episodes where that's the drive, and then it's nice.
有很多集都是这种驱动力，然后感觉很好。

00:19:53.906 --> 00:19:55.609
It's relieving at the end.
结尾时令人释怀。

00:19:55.609 --> 00:19:57.880
Ted surrenders at the end of that episode.
泰德在那集的结尾妥协了。

00:19:57.880 --> 00:20:05.504
He's sitting there with Barney, and he surrenders his preconception about himself that he's the guy that checks I'm going to have a plus one.
他和巴尼坐在一起，他放弃了关于自己是那种会确认“我会带个女伴”的人的固有观念。

00:20:05.504 --> 00:20:06.585
He surrenders Robin.
他放弃了罗宾。

00:20:06.585 --> 00:20:07.667
I'm trying too hard.
我太勉强了。

00:20:07.667 --> 00:20:09.234
I'm working too hard of this.
我对这件事用力过猛了。

00:20:09.234 --> 00:20:13.290
It's a complete moment of surrender, And in that moment, he's given a gift.
这是一个彻底臣服的时刻，而在那一刻，他得到了一份礼物。

00:20:13.289 --> 00:20:14.250
Right, right.
没错，没错。

00:20:14.250 --> 00:20:26.569
And I think part of the complicated math that you and Carter and the writers were doing was like, when is it time for Ted to have a win versus when is it time for him to just slip on the ice or the banana peel?
我认为你、卡特和编剧们所做的复杂计算的一部分是，泰德什么时候该赢一次，什么时候该让他滑倒在冰面或香蕉皮上？

00:20:26.569 --> 00:20:26.809
Yeah.
是的。

00:20:26.809 --> 00:20:27.289
You know?
你知道吗？

00:20:27.289 --> 00:20:27.609
Yeah.
对。

00:20:27.609 --> 00:20:32.170
And he always got a win right when I felt like he needed it.
而且每当我觉得他需要的时候，他总能迎来一场胜利。

00:20:32.170 --> 00:20:37.049
Like I could feel like, oh, thank God he has some wins here, you know?
就好像我能感觉到，哦，谢天谢地，他在这里赢了几场，你知道吗？

00:20:37.049 --> 00:20:38.569
Sometimes you would say that to us.
有时你会对我们这么说。

00:20:38.569 --> 00:20:42.329
You're like, I'm on Ted to stop getting the shit kicked out of him and it's like, you were right.
你会说，我希望泰德别再挨揍了，结果证明，你是对的。

00:20:42.329 --> 00:20:44.752
Whenever you would say that, it was like, he's right.
每当你这么说的时候，就会觉得，他说得对。

00:20:44.752 --> 00:20:46.809
You're the one inhabiting and embodying that.
你是那个深入其中并体现这一点的人。

00:20:46.809 --> 00:20:47.847
You're feeling it.
你感同身受。

00:20:47.847 --> 00:20:48.827
You are that guy.
你就是那个家伙。

00:20:48.827 --> 00:20:50.556
You're really living with Ted.
你真的和泰德合二为一了。

00:20:50.556 --> 00:20:52.170
You're living in Ted's skin.
你活在了泰德的皮肤里。

00:20:52.170 --> 00:20:54.890
And you would often protect Ted in that way.
你经常会以那种方式保护泰德。

00:20:54.890 --> 00:20:59.759
Well, you become a little bit of the guardian or the gatekeeper of him.
嗯，你有点成了他的守护者或把关人。

00:20:59.759 --> 00:21:03.189
And I'm like, I know what you guys are doing here.
而我想的是，我知道你们在搞什么鬼。

00:21:03.189 --> 00:21:18.690
Well, sometimes it was like, I wanted less comedy at Ted's expense and more, we'll get into this and I remember feeling it very strongly after drum roll, which is the episode right after, which is zip, zip, zip, is that right?
嗯，有时我希望少一点拿泰德开玩笑的喜剧成分，多一些……我们待会儿会聊到这个，我记得在“未完待续”（即紧接着的那集，也就是“拉链，拉链，拉链”，是这样吗？）之后，我这种感觉非常强烈。

00:21:18.690 --> 00:21:19.690
Yeah.
对。

00:21:19.690 --> 00:21:20.690
Yeah.
对。

00:21:20.690 --> 00:21:32.849
I remember he's, his romanticism is lionized and heralded in drum roll, please, and then absolutely completely mocked in the next episode and I remember getting whiplash going between the two.
我记得在《请期待》里，他的浪漫主义被奉若神明、大加赞赏，而在下一集里却被彻底嘲弄，我记得这两集之间的反差让我感到头晕目眩。

00:21:32.849 --> 00:21:34.410
I was like, look, is this cute?
我就想，听着，这究竟是可爱呢？

00:21:34.410 --> 00:21:39.730
Do you feel that this is a virtue of his or do you think you wanna mock this?
你觉得这是他的美德，还是你想嘲笑它？

00:21:39.730 --> 00:21:41.970
Like I was really confused about it.
当时我对此真的很困惑。

00:21:41.970 --> 00:21:50.970
Yeah, yeah, in such close proximity too because in general, please is such a freaking hero moment for Ted and yeah, yeah, that's interesting.
对，对，而且两集离得这么近，因为总的来说，《请期待》对泰德来说简直是个英雄高光时刻，对吧，对，这挺有意思的。

00:21:50.970 --> 00:21:51.809
Well, we'll get there.
好吧，我们待会儿会聊到。

00:21:51.809 --> 00:21:53.970
Well, and we'll untie that when we get there.
嗯，等我们到那部分时再来解开这个结。

